Chapter Twenty: The Example of the Workers in the Vineyard
The way to the kingdom of heaven is like a man who had a vineyard; he went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He lent them a dinar for the day and sent them to his gardens.
And at nine in the morning, he went out and found other workers standing in the market without work.
And he says to them: Go until you reach my gardens, and I will pay you what you deserve.
And they left. Inside him, the daytime indifference came out again, and it came out again at three in the afternoon, and more workers came.
And one more time, he went out around five in the afternoon, and found other workers standing, and said to them: Why are you standing here all day without working?
And they answered him: Because no one has employed us. And he said to them: Well then, go until you reach my gardens.
And when evening came, the garden owner told his agent: Call all the workers and give them their pay, starting from the last ones until you reach the first.
Those who rented them at five in the afternoon came, and each one of them took a dinar.
And when the first ones came, they expected to be paid extra, but they also only took one dinar each.
And when they were getting paid, they started complaining about the landlord,
And they say: those others only worked for one hour and you paid them in cash, while we stayed working all day in the sun.
And the king's servant answered one of them, and said to him: I have not wronged you, my friend, didn't we agree on a dinar?
So take your due and go your way! And this is what came in the end, I am the one who wanted to give it to you.
